Under California law, an arbitration clause can be challenged as unconscionable if it is found to be both procedurally and substantively unconscionable. (See Armendariz v. Foundation Health Psychcare Services, Inc. (2000) 24 Cal.4th 83, 114.) 

The fact that the arbitration clause requires the costs to be split 50/50 may be considered substantively unconscionable if it would impose an unfair or oppressive cost burden on one party. (See Gutierrez v. Autowest, Inc. (2003) 114 Cal.App.4th 77, 90-91.) However, California courts have generally upheld cost-splitting provisions in arbitration agreements, as long as they are not unduly burdensome or unfair. (See, e.g., Mercuro v. Superior Court (2002) 96 Cal.App.4th 167, 175-176.)

To challenge the arbitration clause as unconscionable, you would need to show that the cost split is likely to impose an undue burden on you, or that there are other factors that render the clause procedurally or substantively unconscionable.

Some factors that may be considered when evaluating the unconscionability of an arbitration clause include:

1. Whether the clause was presented on a take-it-or-leave-it basis, without opportunity for negotiation (procedural unconscionability);
2. Whether the cost-splitting provision would impose an undue financial burden on you (substantive unconscionability);
3. Whether the arbitration forum (in this case, the American Arbitration Association) is biased or has a history of bias (substantive unconscionability);
4. Whether the clause limits your ability to recover damages or obtain other relief that would be available in a court of law (substantive unconscionability).

To mount a successful challenge, you would need to present evidence and argumentation on these factors, demonstrating that the arbitration clause is both procedurally and substantively unconscionable.

It is recommended that you consult with an attorney who is familiar with California law and arbitration law to assess the specific circumstances and determine the best course of action. They can help you evaluate the enforceability of the arbitration clause and advise on potential challenges.

